    "title": "Brits say ‘why have I got lurgy in summer’ as nation hit by wave of illness - AOL",
    "source": "AOL.co.uk",
    "author": "Ben Hurst",
    "published_at": "2026-08-18T00:00:00",
    "image": "https://hermes.media.static.aol.com/media/2026/08/18/4c2195c6-f3c3-3123-9857-83f608f277bd/e2b63cea-fe4e-4c92-ab13-bb531f941d37.jpg",
    "url": "https://www.aol.co.uk/articles/brits-why-got-lurgy-summer-060122000.html",
    "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMifEFVX3lxTFBvYTdzVllBVnV4UjlGRzdNS3NxNmNsUjA5ZE9OQk41SVJHTHJ3Z1FKRGNkOUFSTWhfLWpaZUhlUklTdXNRMzJ3WXVvN1RzU0dXZTJVV1REV1FrSElkbnp1TWd6LUdhMG40ZkcwUlJ6MUpFWDI2RkVLUEdSU3U?oc=5",
    "content": "![Mirror UK](https://hermes.media.static.aol.com/logos/light/mirror-uk.png?v=1784567839)\n![People in the UK are complaining of a wave of sickness like colds or flu - or even Covid - this summer](https://hermes.media.static.aol.com/media/2026/08/18/4c2195c6-f3c3-3123-9857-83f608f277bd/e2b63cea-fe4e-4c92-ab13-bb531f941d37.jpg)\nPeople are complaining that a ‘summer lurgy’ is sweeping the UK - and fear there’s been a summer surge of Covid. People have taken to social media to complain of coughs, sniffles, sore throats and headaches - and wonder how it can happen in a heatwave.\nGoogle Trends has reported that over the past week, “can you get the flu in summer?” is a ‘breakout’ term, signifying a growth of over 5,000% in search interest. The NHS is under pressure this summer with high attendances in A&E caused probably by the weather which has brought an increase in existing conditions worsening.\nDuring the May and June 2026 heat events the UK Health Security Agency reported an estimated total of 2,877 heat-associated deaths - already near the highest levels ever recorded for a whole year. Respiratory conditions are known to be worstened by the heat.\nHeatwaves also cause people to be more exposed as air conditioning and staying indoors to get out the heat can caused increase spreading.\nIn California and America reports suggest the summer peak of Covid-19 is in fact higher now than the winter. Dr. Erica Pan, director of the California Department of Public Health said: ““Anyone who hasn’t received a 2025-26 dose, or is due for the second dose, is encouraged to get vaccinated now, before the next COVID-19 surge,”\n“Disease is on the rise in California, where we’re seeing increasing cases, increasing test positivity, increasing in our wastewater. This is a good time, actually, to protect yourselves or your family,” Pan told The Times in an interview, “because we anticipate, based on the last couple of years at least, that this may be the larger peak than the winter.”\nOn social media people complained at the cold surge. One said: ”I have that ‘summer flu’ that everybody’s going on about and I can confirm that it’s definitely Covid.” Another added: “how does someone get nasopharyngitis in the summer? how is this even possible!! i’m dying from the heat and now i have to deal with this inflammation and the flu?\"\nOne speculated: “I’m suspecting we’re in the midst of a summer surge. A suspicious number of colleagues had headaches that went on for days. I’m getting over a ear infection and a relative has shingles.”\nFrosty said: “I’m sick of having the flu in the middle of summer lol”. River added: “Currently under the weather and praying I don’t have the flu …. Summer sickness is the absolute worst! Have a good night folks it’s cold meds and sleep for me.”\nA Londoner posted on social media: “Me and all my friends have gotten individually ill in the last week. Seems there is some gross summer cold travelling around at the moment. Haven’t left the house since last weekend, and seems my cold is just getting worse by each day!\n“Feeling very sorry for myself, the heat is not helping, both in the sense of it’s too hot to be ill, and can’t go out in the cool mornings.”\nAnother added: “My team at work has had it this week, passed on at an event we attended.” Another person said: “Yes!!! Took out my entire family for a week!”\nDr Dominic Greenyer, a private doctor at the Health Suite, said: “Throughout the year, there are multiple viruses that come and go, and often, there can be clusters. So certain viruses may cause a cluster of cases, which we may be seeing at the moment in London. COVID is one of many viruses that circulate through the natural population every year.\n“With these viruses, you can’t tell initially what it is because there’s a lot of crossover with symptoms. Most viruses cause symptoms such as muscle aches, high temperatures, and fever; these are just symptoms of feeling unwell. It’s hard to pin down a particular virus without getting tested but as most patients get better with no treatment, they don’t get tested. It’s usually only the patients who are very unwell and are admitted to the hospital who get tested.\n“In 2020, we had a particularly severe strain that obviously caused a lot of health problems across the nation. However, is there something specific we should worry about with summer colds? No, not specifically. It could be viruses like COVID, among other things.\n“General medical advice would be good self-care, get plenty of rest, fluids, a balanced diet, because all these things help the body to heal, regardless of what the cause is.”\nDr Greenyer also noted that most people tend to get better within 10-14 days and require ‘no intervention from medical services’. A pharmacist can give treatment advice and recommend flu remedies.\nThe NHS is urging millions of people to book their flu jab from today as the health service starts winter preparations earlier than ever.\nAs of yesterday (August 17), the national booking system is open so eligible people, including pregnant women and children, can pre-book their flu vaccinations when vaccinations start on September 1.\nThe NHS said it wants to do everything it can to ease winter demand after back-to-back heatwaves have seen staff treat record numbers this summer.\nA&E attendances hit a record 2.5 million attendances in July – and staff experienced the 3 busiest months ever recorded between May and July in emergency departments.\nDespite the record pressure, more patients were seen, treated or discharged within 4 hours than in June, with performance improving to 75.4%.\nFlu season usually peaks in December and January, so the NHS is starting vaccinations in autumn, to ensure those at risk are protected and fewer people are hospitalised.",
    "summary": "People across the UK are complaining of a summer wave of illness, including colds, flu-like symptoms, and fears of a Covid surge during recent heatwaves. Google Trends reported a massive spike in searches asking if people can get the flu in summer, while the NHS faces high A&E attendance pressures. Medical experts note that multiple viruses circulate year-round with overlapping symptoms, and recommend general self-care and rest since most people recover without medical intervention.",
    "key_points": [
      "UK residents have taken to social media to report a wave of summer colds, headaches, sore throats, and suspected Covid cases.",
      "Google Trends showed a breakout increase in searches for 'can you get the flu in summer?'",
      "The NHS experienced high A&E attendances and record emergency department demand driven partly by worsening conditions during heatwaves.",
      "Medical experts state that various circulating viruses share similar symptoms and most patients recover with self-care and rest within 10 to 14 days.",
      "The NHS has opened early bookings for autumn flu vaccinations to help ease demand and protect vulnerable populations."
